# Minecraft Version(s): 26.3
## 1.1.0
- Works with the Nice Control Center Panel (Docker): when the panel runs the server, the dashboard listens only inside the container, the panel shows it in its Dashboard tab with its own accounts and permissions, and /ncc web links open the panel
- The dashboard follows the panel's light/dark switch and drops its own header when shown inside the panel

## 1.0.6
- The live TPS badge in the header keeps its width when the value changes (e.g. 20.0 → 19.9)

## 1.0.5
- The TPS, MSPT, CPU, Memory, entity, chunk and player tiles show the current second (updated every second) instead of the chosen time window; the window's average is the small line underneath. GC stays a window value
- Charts scroll smoothly out at the left edge instead of the oldest part being cut off

## 1.0.4
- Answers to admin messages pop up as a notification on every tab (click to open the conversation), with the count in the browser tab title
- Player inspector: the last grave shows who looted it (highlighted if it wasn't the owner) or that it expired, instead of just "removed"

## 1.0.3
- The Memory tile is laid out like MSPT: in use after GC, with garbage not collected yet (both color-coded), and the maximum

## 1.0.2
- \n typed into the MOTD in the server.properties editor is saved as a real second line instead of the text "\n"
- New GC tile next to Memory: share of time paused for garbage collection, number of pauses and the average pause
- "Close to its limit" only shows when more than 2% of ticks run over budget, not because of a handful of slow ticks (autosaves) while the server keeps up easily

## 1.0.1
- Packs installed as mods show their real name in data pack settings and elsewhere (Warping Wonders instead of "wawo")
- Data pack settings also find packs that keep their settings in their own storage, like Katters Structures
- The server name in the dashboard and in reports shows the MOTD's colors and formatting instead of the raw § codes

## First release
- Live monitor that starts with the server: TPS, MSPT (min/med/95%ile/max), CPU, memory, entities, chunks and players for the last 1, 5, 15 and 60 minutes
- "What's costing performance": plain-language findings with the cause and a tip, all in ms per tick
- Data pack functions timed down to single command lines, mods found with a stack sampler, entity and block entity types, busiest chunks and lag spikes with their causes
- World generation cost per step, structure and feature, grouped by data pack or mod
- Player view, redstone clock finder, settings advisor and bloat check
- Lag alerts in chat and automatic lag reports, plus manual recordings as shareable HTML reports and a before/after comparison
- Data pack settings from in-game settings menus, editable in the dashboard (same options and limits as the menu)
- In-game date and time in the dashboard header (Nice Actions calendar or the Minecraft day counter)
- Web dashboard in the Explorer's Eden style with light and dark mode, /ncc commands, an in-game boss bar and LuckPerms permission nodes
- Errors & warnings from the server log, grouped and credited to data packs and mods, with a chat message after a /reload that broke something
- Console in the dashboard: live log and a command line
- Automatic updates for mods and data packs from Modrinth and GitHub, installed at restart, with backups and rollback (/ncc updates)
- Dashboard split into tabs
- Server resource pack kept up to date from a link, GitHub or Modrinth, with the SHA-1 worked out by the mod
- server.properties editor in the dashboard
- Live player inspector: online and offline players, inventories, homes, graves, waypoints and claims
- Gamerule editor in the dashboard
- Data pack settings laid out like the packs' in-game menus, with search
- In-game settings screen through Mod Menu (singleplayer)
- Smoother live charts and in-game clock; calmer layout with a compact header on every tab but the Overview
- Scheduled commands: once, daily, on weekdays or every few minutes, several commands per task
- Private messages with players (they answer with /nccreply), ban and unban in the player inspector
- Scheduled tasks can be chained (run after another task, with a delay), and commands can be spaced out with wait lines or a pause
- Player inspector shows each player's client and recognised mods, and an x-ray hint from mining statistics
